{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"LIVE TIER RAN IN CI (GitHub Actions run 30604756524, job 'jira DC harness smoke') — and it FOUND A\nREAL DEFECT. Result: 2 failed, 12 passed, 1 error.\n\nTHE HEADLINE: a WRITING reconcile pass against a real Jira DC instance CRASHES.\n\n    ERROR: reconcile_once raised: 'JiraDataCenterTransport' object has no attribute\n    'set_entity_property'\n\ndry-run passes (my test_dc_reconcile_pass_raises_no_unhandled_exception is green); only the writing\npath dies. Filed as [rebar:a357-b747-ece9-4cf5].\n\nROOT CAUSE, which is the interesting part: set_entity_property is called from THREE core sites\n(dispatch_one.py:321, binding_store.py:517, apply_inbound_records.py:279), implemented in exactly ONE\n(adapters/jira/acli_rest.py:266, Cloud) — and is NOT A MEMBER OF THE TicketTransport PROTOCOL. The\nport declares only create_issue/get_issue/update_issue/transition_issue_by_name/add_label/\nsearch_issues. So the core depends on a transport method the port never states.\n\nThat is why everything I did this story passed while the real thing was broken: isinstance(x,\nTicketTransport) is satisfied without it, the contract suite never calls it, and 1636 unit tests are\ngreen. Making the DC backend Backend-conformant — this story's core work — was necessary and is NOT\nsufficient, because the Protocol is not a faithful statement of what the core requires. Conformance to\nan incomplete port proves less than it appears to. Same class as J6's add_label defect.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS STORY'S ACs: the three [operator-attested] live criteria are NOT met and I am not\nmarking them met:\n  - full reconcile pass converges + repeated pass writes nothing  -> FAILED (exit 1)\n  - DC-created ticket provenance                                   -> FAILED (same crash)\n  - zero unhandled exceptions                                      -> PASSED for dry-run only\nThe epic cannot honestly claim 'Jira Server/DC support' while a DC deployment cannot complete a\nwriting pass.\n\nSECOND FINDING, and this one MY tests caused: [rebar:f391-806b-938f-45d8]. The run also errored at\nsetup of an UNRELATED, previously-passing test with\n'UserTokenLimitExceededException: You can't create more than 10 tokens'. jira_dc_pat\n(conftest.py:355) is FUNCTION-scoped with no teardown, so every consuming test mints a new PAT and\nnever deletes it; Jira DC caps a user at 10. My three new tests transitively request it and pushed the\nsession over. The bug was latent; my tests made it fire — and it surfaces in a module nobody touched,\nwhich is exactly the misattribution that burns a debugging session.\n\nWHAT WENT RIGHT: this is the live tier doing precisely its job. Every unit-level signal was green; only\ndriving a real instance exposed it. That is the argument for this story having had a live AC at all."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785473579750438001,"uuid":"a3cf08b9-9a90-4a5b-8584-5cc7ba911104"}